  "why": "The authors' 1,000-draw simulation result is not reproducible without their exact simulation code and random seed. Reimplementing the stated method (1,000 binomial draws of each station's song count at its observed share of women's songs, counting adjacent women-women pairs) puts 24-25 of the 29 stations below the 2.5th percentile, not 17. The observed per-station women's back-to-back counts themselves are reproduced in the station-share fact."
 },
 {
  "source": "For back-to-back songs by men, it was 4,231 (65.53%).",
  "why": "The count is reproduced (4,231 of 6,474), but 4,231/6,474 = 65.35%, not 65.53%; the stated percentage appears to transpose the digits. The included KCYY fact uses the computed 65.35%."
 },
 {
  "source": "The top 3 current women artists make up 52% of current airplay for women.",
  "why": "The individual shares are reproduced (23.2%, 17.0%, 12.0%), but the chart's total of 52.5% is not: the three shares sum to 52.2% and the text says 52%. The included fact reports the computed 52.2%."
 },
 {
  "source": "The top 3 men account for 20.3% of current airplay for men.",
  "why": "The individual shares and the text's 20.3% are reproduced, but the chart's total of 20.4% is not: the three shares sum to 20.3%. The included fact uses 20.3%."
 },
 {
  "source": "in 2022 songs by Black women received just 0.03% of radio airplay — with spins almost entirely in the evenings and overnights.",
  "why": "plays.csv covers 19 dates in 2022, not the full year, and gives only 14 spins by Black women (0.008% of its songs), not 0.03%. The 0.03% figure comes from broader 2022 airplay data not supplied in this folder."
 },
 {
  "source": "The same dire statistics are true for LGBTQIA+ artists, who received 0.13% of the airplay in 2022.",
